post_install() {
  cat <<'MSG'

passman is installed but not yet serving anything. It does not take over your
session's secret store on its own — that would point every application at an
empty vault.

  1. Create a vault and import what your current keyring holds:
       passman-cli init
       passman-cli import

  2. Take over org.freedesktop.secrets for your user:
       systemctl --user enable --now passman-daemon.service

     The unit starts locked and waits. To have it unlocked at login, add
     pam_passman.so to your login stack — see
     /usr/share/doc/passman-git/pam-install.md

  3. Check what is actually wired up at any time from the app's Settings page.

Nothing here has been reviewed by anyone but its author. Read
/usr/share/doc/passman-git/SECURITY.md before trusting it with anything you
cannot afford to lose.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  # The daemon holds the vault key in memory; a new binary is only in use once
  # it restarts, and restarting it locks the vault.
  cat <<'MSG'

passman was upgraded. Restart the daemon when convenient:

    systemctl --user restart passman-daemon.service

It starts locked, so you will be asked for your passphrase again.

MSG
}
